  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 210 PM Friday... \n\nKEY MESSAGE 1... A brief warmup this weekend, with showers expected \nahead of a cold front late Sunday and Sunday night.\n\nAloft, a nrn stream s/w, which is expected to move onshore over the \nPacific Northwest this evening, will track esewd across the Rockies \nand cntl Plains through Sat night. The s/w trough will amplify as it \ncontinues ewd across the Midwest on Sun, perhaps taking a slight \nnegative tilt as it moves across the OH Valley, mid-Atlantic, and \nNortheast US late Sun into Mon. At the surface, a low pressure \nsystem will develop over the srn Plains Sat/Sat night, while high \npressure moves ewd across FL. The low will then track ewd-newd \nacross the mid-MS and OH Valley regions on Sun and the ern Great \nLakes and Northeast Sun night/Mon. Meanwhile, a warm front should \nlift across cntl NC Sun aft/eve, immediately ahead of the attendant \ncold front, which should move ewd across the area Sun night. If the \nparent low begins to occlude as it tracks across the OH Valley/ern \nGreat Lakes, a secondary triple point low may develop east of the \nAppalachians over wrn VA/NC, but confidence in that development is \nstill somewhat low. There could be a brief window of some higher \ntheta-e advection into the area Sun evening, but GFS forecast \nsoundings still show little to no instability. However, the \nwind/shear profiles remain robust, with a rapidly strengthening low \nand mid-level jet over the area as the base of the trough swings \nacross. The 12Z operational GFS has 40-55 kts at 925 mb and 65-75 \nkts at 850 mb between 03Z and 09Z Mon. PWATs should also increase \nbriefly to 1.2 to 1.5 inches NW to SE ahead of the cold front. For \nnow, there is still a high probability for showers with minimal \nthunder potential, along and ahead of the front Sun eve through Sun \nnight/early Mon. It may also be quite breezy, with the potential for \nsome strong gusts if any deeper convection develops given the strong \nwinds aloft. However, for now there is too much uncertainty/low \nconfidence given the lack of instability and relatively weak low-\nlevel lapse rates. The system should be fairly quick to move across \nthe area, with rain ending by Monday morning, thus rainfall amounts \nshould be limited, generally half an inch or less.\n\nTemperatures: This weekend should be mild, with highs in the upper \n50s to mid 60s. Lows Sun night forecast in the 40s, based on current \nfropa timing, which is above to well above normal (5-15 degrees). A \nreturn to near to below normal temperatures is expected early to mid \nnext week as high pressure builds into and sits over the region.",
